    "text": "<p>An observer can see through a small hole on the side of a jar (radius <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>15</mn><mo>&#160;</mo><mi>cm</mi></math> ) at a point at height of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>15</mn><mo>&#160;</mo><mi>cm</mi></math>&nbsp;from the bottom (see figure). The hole is at a height of&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>45</mn><mo>&#160;</mo><mi>cm</mi></math>. When the jar is filled with a liquid up to a height of&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>30</mn><mo>&#160;</mo><mi>cm</mi></math> the same observer can see the edge at the bottom of the jar. If the refractive index of the liquid is<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mfrac><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">N</mi><mn>100</mn></mfrac><mo>,</mo></math> where&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">N</mi></math> is an integer, the value of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">N</mi></math> is</p><p><img src=\"assets\\qt0165_8ce01f4228d9.png\" style=\"width: 253px; height: 248px;\" /></p>"
  },
